.TH "CSSMATHCLAMP" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathClamp \- CSSMathClamp
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSMathClamp\f[B]\f[R] interface of the CSS Typed Object
Model API represents the CSS \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] function.
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathClamp()\f[R]
Creates a new \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] object.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
\f[I]Also inherits properties from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathClamp.lower\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] object containing the minimum
value.
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathClamp.value\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] object containing the preferred
value.
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathClamp.upper\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] object containing the upper value.
.SH STATIC METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The CSS \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] function takes three arguments: a minimum,
preferred, and maximum value, and returns the preferred value, clamped
between the minimum and maximum.
.PP
If all three arguments are absolute values, such as pixel lengths,
\f[CR]clamp()\f[R] is resolved to a single value at parse time,
represented by the CSS Typed Object Model as a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R].
If the \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] expression can\(cqt be resolved to a single
value at parse time (say, because one of its arguments uses a relative
unit like \f[CR]vw\f[R] or \f[CR]%\f[R]), the function is represented as
a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] object, and the three arguments passed to
\f[CR]clamp()\f[R] (or to the \f[CR]CSSMathClamp()\f[R] constructor) are
exposed as the \f[CR]lower\f[R], \f[CR]value\f[R], and \f[CR]upper\f[R]
properties.
.PP
Note that \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] represents the \f[CR]clamp()\f[R]
function, not its resolved value.
In order to determine the value of a clamped property, you need to read
its computed style (for example with \f[CR]getComputedStyle()\f[R]).
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] instance from three
lengths, then reads back its \f[CR]lower\f[R], \f[CR]value\f[R], and
\f[CR]upper\f[R] properties.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] clamp = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathClamp(CSS.px(10), CSS.percent(50), CSS.px(500));

console.log(clamp.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathClamp\(dq\f[R]
console.log(clamp.lower); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(clamp.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 50, unit: \(dqpercent\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(clamp.upper); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 500, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
.EE
.SS \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] representations
This example shows how \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] is represented by a
\f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] or a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R], depending on
whether all of its arguments are absolute values.
.SS HTML
First we declare a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element, \f[CR]#demoBox\f[R], on
which we\(cqll set some clamped properties.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdemoBox\(dq>Text</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The \f[CR]width\f[R] of the box is set using a \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] whose
three arguments are all absolute lengths, so the browser can resolve it
to a single fixed value immediately.
\f[CR]font\-size\f[R] is set using a \f[CR]clamp()\f[R] whose preferred
value uses the relative unit \f[CR]vw\f[R], so the browser can\(cqt
resolve it until layout (this will be represented by a
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
demoBox {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: clamp(10px, 50px, 500px);
  \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: clamp(1rem, 5vw, 3rem);
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
First we find the demo box\(cqs style rule and read its \f[CR]width\f[R]
and \f[CR]font\-size\f[R] values using \f[CR]styleMap\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] demoBox = document.querySelector(\(dq#demoBox\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] rules = document.getElementById(\(dqcss\-output\(dq).sheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] rule = [...rules].find((r) \f[B]=>\f[R] r.selectorText === \(dq#demoBox\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] styleMap = rule.styleMap;
\f[B]const\f[R] width = styleMap.get(\(dqwidth\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] fontSize = styleMap.get(\(dqfont\-size\(dq);
.EE
.PP
We then log the type and value of the CSS Typed OM representations,
followed by the computed (resolved) values.
.IP
.EX
log(\(dqwidth\(dq);
log(\(ga type: ${width.constructor.name}\(ga);
log(\(ga value: ${width}\(ga);
log(\(ga resolved: ${getComputedStyle(demoBox).width}\(ga);

log(\(dq\(rsnfont\-size\(dq);
log(\(ga type: ${fontSize.constructor.name}\(ga);
log(\(ga lower: ${fontSize.lower}\(ga);
log(\(ga value: ${fontSize.value}\(ga);
log(\(ga upper: ${fontSize.upper}\(ga);
log(\(ga resolved: ${getComputedStyle(demoBox).fontSize}\(ga);
.EE
.SS Result
\f[CR]width\f[R] logs as a single \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R], and its
resolved value matches that value directly.
\f[CR]font\-size\f[R] logs as a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R], exposing the
\f[CR]clamp()\f[R] function\(cqs original operands.
.SS Inspecting a clamped value
This example uses three range sliders to set the \f[CR]lower\f[R],
\f[CR]preferred\f[R], and \f[CR]upper\f[R] values of a
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R], then applies it to the width of a box using
\f[CR]attributeStyleMap.set()\f[R].
This allows you to see the effect of changing the range on the clamped
value of the \f[CR]width\f[R].
.PP
Dragging a slider changes what \f[CR]lower\f[R], \f[CR]value\f[R], and
\f[CR]upper\f[R] report, because they always mirror the three operands
passed to the \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] \(em note that \f[CR]value\f[R] is
reported in \f[CR]vw\f[R], not the pixels shown on its slider.
The output next to the preferred slider shows both its pixel value and
the \f[CR]vw\f[R] equivalent actually passed to the constructor, so the
conversion stays visible.
The box\(cqs actual rendered width, by contrast, is the result of
clamping that \f[CR]vw\f[R] value between the two pixel bounds, and can
differ substantially from \f[CR]value\f[R] itself \(em for example, when
the preferred slider is dragged below the lower slider or above the
upper slider.
.SS HTML
First we define a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element for the resizable box, three
sliders to set the minimum, preferred, and upper values of its width,
and \f[CR]<output>\f[R] elements to display the slider values
numerically.
All three sliders share the same 0 to 400 pixel range, so their
positions are directly comparable.
We set the initial values so that \f[CR]lower < pref < upper\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqbox\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqcontrols\(dq>
  <\f[B]label\f[R] for=\(dqlower\(dq>Lower (px)</\f[B]label\f[R]>
  <\f[B]input\f[R] id=\(dqlower\(dq type=\(dqrange\(dq min=\(dq0\(dq max=\(dq400\(dq value=\(dq50\(dq />
  <\f[B]output\f[R] for=\(dqlower\(dq id=\(dqlowerOut\(dq></\f[B]output\f[R]>

  <\f[B]label\f[R] for=\(dqpref\(dq>Preferred (px)</\f[B]label\f[R]>
  <\f[B]input\f[R] id=\(dqpref\(dq type=\(dqrange\(dq min=\(dq0\(dq max=\(dq400\(dq value=\(dq180\(dq />
  <\f[B]output\f[R] for=\(dqpref\(dq id=\(dqprefOut\(dq></\f[B]output\f[R]>

  <\f[B]label\f[R] for=\(dqupper\(dq>Upper (px)</\f[B]label\f[R]>
  <\f[B]input\f[R] id=\(dqupper\(dq type=\(dqrange\(dq min=\(dq0\(dq max=\(dq400\(dq value=\(dq350\(dq />
  <\f[B]output\f[R] for=\(dqupper\(dq id=\(dqupperOut\(dq></\f[B]output\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
<\f[B]pre\f[R] id=\(dqlog\(dq></\f[B]pre\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
At the end we define a \f[CR]#log\f[R] element to output information
returned about the box width.
.SS CSS
The CSS sets the visual properties and alignment of the box, sliders,
and other elements.
.IP
.EX
box {
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 50px;
  \f[B]background\f[R]: rebeccapurple;
}

\&.controls {
  \f[B]display\f[R]: grid;
  \f[B]grid\-template\-columns\f[R]: auto 1fr auto;
  \f[B]align\-items\f[R]: center;
  \f[B]gap\f[R]: 0.5rem 1rem;
  \f[B]max\-width\f[R]: 400px;
}

\&.controls output {
  \f[B]font\-family\f[R]: monospace;
  \f[B]text\-align\f[R]: right;
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
First we create variables to reference the box, the sliders, and the
output elements.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] box = document.querySelector(\(dq#box\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] lowerInput = document.querySelector(\(dq#lower\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] prefInput = document.querySelector(\(dq#pref\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] upperInput = document.querySelector(\(dq#upper\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] lowerOut = document.querySelector(\(dq#lowerOut\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] prefOut = document.querySelector(\(dq#prefOut\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] upperOut = document.querySelector(\(dq#upperOut\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Then we call the \f[CR]update()\f[R] function to update the box and
output elements based on the slider value.
We set up a listener so that the function is called whenever the slider
positions are changed.
.IP
.EX
[lowerInput, prefInput, upperInput].forEach((el) \f[B]=>\f[R]
  el.addEventListener(\(dqinput\(dq, update),
);
update();
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]update()\f[R] function is shown below.
This logs the values of the sliders and uses them when creating a
\f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] that is then set on the \f[CR]width\f[R]
attribute of the box.
The attribute styles of the box are then read using
\f[CR]HTMLElement.attributeStyleMap\f[R] and the retrieved values of
\f[CR]width\f[R] are also logged, along with the rendered width of the
box.
.PP
One complexity in the code is that while \f[CR]lower\f[R] and
\f[CR]upper\f[R] are passed to the \f[CR]CSSMathClamp()\f[R] constructor
as pixels, exactly matching their sliders, the pixel value of
\f[CR]preferred\f[R] is first converted to \f[CR]vw\f[R] (viewport
width) units.
This has been done because if all three operands were absolute lengths
(for example, all in pixels), the browser could resolve
\f[CR]clamp()\f[R] down to a single fixed number, which would be read
back as a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] instead of a \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R].
Converting \f[CR]preferred\f[R] to a relative unit like \f[CR]vw\f[R]
means the browser can\(cqt resolve the expression until layout, so it
keeps the value as a live \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R] with all three
operands intact.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] update() {
  logElement.innerText = \(dq\(dq;

  \f[I]// The preferred slider uses the same 0\-400px scale as lower and upper,\f[R]
  \f[I]// so its value is converted to vw before being passed to CSSMathClamp.\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] prefVw = (prefInput.value / window.innerWidth) * 100;
  lowerOut.textContent = \(ga${lowerInput.value}px\(ga;
  prefOut.textContent = \(ga${prefInput.value}px (\(ti${prefVw.toFixed(1)}vw)\(ga;
  upperOut.textContent = \(ga${upperInput.value}px\(ga;

  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] clampValue = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathClamp(
      CSS.px(lowerInput.value),
      CSS.vw(prefVw),
      CSS.px(upperInput.value),
    );
    box.attributeStyleMap.set(\(dqwidth\(dq, clampValue);
    \f[B]const\f[R] widthClamp = box.attributeStyleMap.get(\(dqwidth\(dq);
    \f[B]const\f[R] valuePx = (widthClamp.value.value / 100) * window.innerWidth;
    log(\(gatype: ${widthClamp.constructor.name}\(ga);
    log(\(galower: ${widthClamp.lower}\(ga);
    log(\(gavalue: ${widthClamp.value} (\(ti${valuePx.toFixed(1)}px)\(ga);
    log(\(gaupper: ${widthClamp.upper}\(ga);
    log(\(garendered width: ${getComputedStyle(box).width}\(ga);
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    log(\(gaError: ${e.message}\(ga);
  }
}
.EE
.SS Result
Drag the sliders to see how \f[CR]lower\f[R], \f[CR]value\f[R], and
\f[CR]upper\f[R] always match the slider positions, while the rendered
width is clamped between \f[CR]lower\f[R] and \f[CR]upper\f[R].
